course: biology
lesson: 46
title: Food chains: sun, plants, plant-eaters, meat-eaters
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Almost every meal on Earth starts with sunlight: green plants and algae catch the Sun's energy and build food from water and carbon dioxide, and every animal lives by eating plants or eating something that ate plants.
explain: A food chain shows who eats whom: grass, then rabbit, then fox, with arrows pointing from the food to the eater, the way the energy goes.
explain: Look at your cheese sandwich: the bread was wheat that grew in sunshine, the cheese came from milk from a cow that ate grass, so you are at the end of food chains too.
explain: At each step most of the energy is used up for moving, breathing and keeping warm, so there are always far more zebras than lions, and a cartoon savanna with as many lions as zebras could never last a single season.
fact: Plants make their own food from sunlight, water and carbon dioxide; this is photosynthesis, it uses the green chlorophyll in leaves and gives out oxygen; plants do not eat soil, which only gives them water and minerals.
fact: In a food chain such as grass, rabbit, fox, the grass is the producer, the rabbit a plant-eater or herbivore, and the fox a meat-eater or carnivore; arrows point from food to eater.
fact: At each step only about a tenth of the energy passes on to the next eater, so there are far more plants than plant-eaters and far more plant-eaters than meat-eaters, and chains are usually only 3 to 5 steps long.
fact: A pond food chain can be tiny green algae, water fleas, sticklebacks, then a heron or a pike; a jar of pond water can hold thousands of living things.
fact: The blue whale, the biggest animal that has ever lived, eats almost only krill, small shrimp-like animals about 5 cm long that eat drifting algae, so its chain is very short and little energy is lost, and can eat several tonnes of them in a day.
fact: Tiny drifting algae and bacteria called phytoplankton are the producers of the ocean, and they make roughly half of the oxygen produced on Earth.
fact: Blue tits time their chicks to hatch when caterpillars are most common, and one family can eat thousands of caterpillars, often quoted as about 10,000.
fact: A real fox eats rabbits, mice, birds, worms, beetles, fruit and leftovers, so food chains join into a tangled food web, which lets animals switch food when one runs short.
fact: Wolves were brought back to Yellowstone in 1995; elk numbers fell and in some places young willows and aspens grew back, though scientists still argue how big the wolves' effect really was.
fact: Sea otters eat sea urchins, which eat kelp; where otters live kelp forests grow tall, and without them urchins can chew kelp down to bare rock.
fact: Near hot springs on the deep sea floor, found in 1977, food chains start with bacteria that make food from chemicals instead of sunlight; giant tube worms have no mouth and live on such bacteria inside them.
fact: People are omnivores, eating plants and animals, and growing plants for people uses much less land than feeding plants to animals and eating the animals.
exercise: Where does the energy in almost every food chain come from first? | The Sun | The soil | The rain | The animals at the top
exercise: What do plants need to make their own food? | Sunlight, water and carbon dioxide from the air | Soil, which they eat up slowly through their roots | Only water and warmth | Insects that fall on their leaves
exercise: Which gas do plants give out when they make food? | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Smoke | Steam only
exercise: In the chain grass, rabbit, fox, which one is the producer? | The grass | The rabbit | The red fox | None of them
exercise: Which way do the arrows point in a food chain drawing? | From the food to the eater, the way the energy goes | From the eater to the food | From the biggest animal down to the smallest one | Always from left to right on the page, whatever eats what
exercise: Why are there far more zebras than lions on the savanna? | At each step most energy is used up, so only a little passes on | Lions do not like having many babies, so they only have one each | Zebras eat lions when they are young | Lions eat only once in their whole lives
exercise: About how much of the energy passes on at each step of a food chain? | About a tenth | All of it, every bit | About half of it | None of it at all
exercise: What do water fleas in a pond eat? | Tiny green algae | Big fish and frogs | Herons that wade in the pond | Only the mud at the bottom
exercise: What does a blue whale mostly eat? | Krill, small shrimp-like animals | Big fish and seals it chases down | Seaweed from the bottom | Other whales
exercise: Why can a blue whale grow so huge on tiny food? | It eats near the start of a very short chain, so little energy is lost | Krill contain far more energy than meat or fish, gram for gram | It eats only once a year | It also makes food from sunlight like a plant, through its skin
exercise: What are phytoplankton? | Tiny drifting algae and bacteria that make food from sunlight | Tiny fish eggs that float near the top of the sea in spring | Small jellyfish | Grains of sand that float and drift
exercise: What do blue tit parents mostly feed their chicks? | Caterpillars | Seeds from the bird table | Bread thrown by people | Worms from the lawn
exercise: Why is a food web a better picture than a single chain? | Most animals eat many foods and are eaten by many predators | Food webs are prettier to draw | Animals never eat more than one kind of food in their whole lives | Spiders build them around the chain
exercise: What happened in some places after wolves came back to Yellowstone? | Elk numbers fell and young willows and aspens grew back | The forest died because the wolves chewed the bark off the trees | The wolves ate all the bears | Nothing changed at all
exercise: What happens to kelp forests where sea otters disappear? | Sea urchins multiply and chew the kelp down | The kelp grows even taller and thicker than before | The kelp turns into grass | The urchins leave to look for otters
exercise: How do food chains start at deep sea hot springs, where sunlight never reaches? | Bacteria make food from chemicals in the hot water | Tube worms make food from the light that glowing fish give off | Food falls from ships | Plants grow with the heat instead of light
exercise: Why are people called omnivores? | We eat both plants and animals | We eat only meat | We eat only plants | We make our own food from sunlight
test: «food chain: fox → rabbit → grass, cos the fox is the boss» What is wrong? | Arrows go from food to eater: grass to rabbit to fox | Nothing, that is right | The grass should be at the top because it is the biggest thing in the field | The rabbit eats the fox
test: In a cartoon a lion eats grass with the zebras every day. Where does a real lion get its energy? | From eating plant-eaters, which got it from plants and the Sun | From the grass, like the zebras, only it eats a lot more of it | From the sunshine on its mane | From the water it drinks
test: «why isn't there a super-lion that eats lions» What is the best answer? | Too little energy is left at the top of a chain to feed it | Lions are too fast and too strong for anything to catch | Lions are poisonous | There is one, but it hides really well in caves
test: What would happen to the foxes if a disease killed most of the rabbits in a field? | They would eat more mice, beetles and fruit, or go hungry | Nothing, foxes do not eat rabbits | The foxes would start eating grass only, like the rabbits used to | There would be more foxes than before
test: «mate reckons plants eat the soil to grow, that's why they need it» What is right? | No, plants make food from light, air and water; soil gives water and minerals | Yes, plants eat soil | Yes, the roots chew the soil up slowly and swallow it bit by bit, mate | No, plants need nothing at all to grow
test: Where does the energy in your cheese sandwich come from in the end? | The Sun, through wheat and through grass eaten by a cow | The fridge | The electricity used by the cheese factory and the bakery | The salt in the bread
test: «sea's just water, it doesn't make oxygen» What is right? | Phytoplankton in the sea make roughly half of Earth's oxygen | Right, all of Earth's oxygen comes from the rainforests on land | Right, the sea uses up all oxygen | Oxygen comes only from volcanoes
test: «why'd they bring wolves back, they'll just eat stuff» What did scientists see? | Elk grazed differently and some young trees grew back, though how much is argued | The wolves ate every single animal in the park, then started on each other | The wolves made the rivers dry up | Nothing at all happened
test: A sea otter floats on its back cracking a sea urchin. Why is that good for the kelp forest? | Fewer urchins means less kelp gets eaten | Otters plant new kelp using their little stones | The broken urchin shells sink and feed the kelp | It is not, because otters eat lots of kelp too
test: «a whole field of wheat feeds more people than the same field feeding cows, why» What is right? | Energy is lost at each step, so eating plants directly wastes less | Cows eat less than people | Wheat has more energy than any meat ever could, so it fills you up more | Cows make energy from sunlight
test: «deep sea worms live on sunlight too, everything does» What is right? | No, at hot vents bacteria make food from chemicals, not light | Yes, sunlight reaches the deepest sea | Yes, the worms glow in the dark and make their own little bit of sunlight | No, deep sea worms do not eat at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the process of photosynthesis? | The making by plants of food from water and carbon dioxide by sunlight, releasing oxygen | The absorption by plants of food already present in the soil, by means of the roots | The consumption by plants of insects for nourishment | The conversion by plants of oxygen into soil
exam: Identify the principal reason for which food chains are seldom longer than five steps. | At each step most energy is expended, so too little remains for further steps | Predators are prohibited by their nature from consuming other predators | Plants cease to grow when a chain exceeds five steps | Animals at the summit of a chain require no food
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the diet of the blue whale. | It subsists almost entirely upon krill, small crustaceans which eat drifting algae | It subsists principally upon seals, squid and large fish pursued at great depth | It subsists upon seaweed torn from the sea floor | It subsists upon other species of whale
exam: Ascertain the origin of food in the communities surrounding hot springs upon the deep sea floor. | Bacteria which make food from chemicals dissolved in the heated water | Sunlight which penetrates to the greatest depths | Plants which flourish in darkness by means of the heat of the springs | Food discarded from vessels upon the surface
